data.source:
`@mortar-kit/auth` protects the admin. **Sign-in methods** (Google, an emailed link or code, or your own) prove who someone is. Your **`authorize`** function then decides whether they may in, and as which user. A signed cookie keeps them signed in.

It runs on the server only: import it from middleware and server functions, never from client code.

## Set it up

The quickest way is `authFromEnv`, which reads its settings from environment variables:

```ts
// src/server/auth.ts
import { authFromEnv } from '@mortar-kit/auth';
import { createJsonFileStore } from '@mortar-kit/core/server';

export const auth = authFromEnv({ store: createJsonFileStore({ dir: '.data' }), siteName: 'My site' });
```

| Variable | What it does |
| --- | --- |
| `MORTAR_AUTH_SECRET` | Signs the session cookie: at least 32 characters (`openssl rand -base64 32`). Comma-separate several to rotate keys. |
| `MORTAR_ADMINS` | Addresses and `*@domain`s that may sign in, comma-separated (unless you pass `authorize`). |
| `GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID`, `GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET` | Turn on Sign in with Google. |
| `RESEND_API_KEY`, `MORTAR_EMAIL_FROM` | Send sign-in emails through Resend, from that address. |
| `MORTAR_BASE_URL` | The public origin, behind a proxy. |

Email sign-in needs `store` (where pending sign-ins wait) and a mailer: Resend from the variables above, or your own `mailer` option. In development (Vite's dev server), a missing secret falls back to a fixed one, `admin@example.com` may sign in, and emails print to the console. A production build never falls back: it refuses to start without a secret, someone who may sign in, and at least one way to sign in. Every option of `createAuth` can be passed too, and wins over the environment.

### By hand

`createAuth` takes everything explicitly:

```ts
import { allowEmails, createAuth, email, google } from '@mortar-kit/auth';
import { createJsonFileStore } from '@mortar-kit/core/server';

export const auth = createAuth({
  methods: [
    google({ clientId: process.env.GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID!, clientSecret: process.env.GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET! }),
    email({ mailer, store: createJsonFileStore({ dir: '.data' }) }),
  ],
  authorize: allowEmails(['you@example.com', '*@example.com']),
  secret: process.env.MORTAR_AUTH_SECRET!,
});
```

`methods` is a list: the sign-in page offers each, in order. `secret` signs the session cookie: at least 32 characters, from the environment. Pass several to rotate keys; the first signs and all are accepted.

## Who may sign in

`authorize` gets the identity a method verified (`{ email, name?, method }`) and returns your user, or `null` to refuse. Look users up however you like:

```ts
async authorize({ identity }) {
  const admin = await db.admins.find(identity.email);
  return admin ? { email: admin.email, name: admin.name } : null;
}
```

It runs when someone signs in and again on every guarded request, so removing someone takes effect at once. Keep it to one quick lookup, and return plain JSON, since the user reaches the admin in the browser. `allowEmails([...])` is a ready-made one for a fixed list of addresses and `*@domain`s.

To manage who may sign in from the admin itself, keep them in a collection of your own and use `allowMembers`:

```ts
export const members = defineCollection({
  name: 'members',
  label: 'Members',
  fields: { email: fields.text({ label: 'Email', required: true }), name: fields.text({ label: 'Name' }) },
});

authFromEnv({ store, authorize: allowMembers({ content, collection: 'members' }) });
```

It lets in anyone whose address (the `email` field; `emailField` to change it) is in a document of the collection, and uses its `name` field as their name. Deleting the document locks them out at once.

## Guard the routes

Put `auth.handle` first in your middleware. It answers the sign-in routes (under `/admin/auth`) and sends signed-out visits to `/admin` and below to the sign-in page:

```ts
// src/middleware.ts (vite.config: solid({ start: { middleware: './src/middleware.ts' } }))
import type { StartMiddleware } from '@solidjs/vite-plugin';
import { auth } from './server/auth.ts';

const middleware: StartMiddleware = async (event, next) => (await auth.handle(event.request)) ?? next();
export default middleware;
```

Middleware can't tell which server-function calls come from the admin, so every function that writes checks for itself. `auth.require` returns the user, or throws a 401 (or a 403 for a cross-site call):

```ts
'use server';
export async function saveDoc(collection: string, doc: Doc) {
  await auth.require(getRequestEvent()!.request);
  return content.save(collection, doc);
}
```

## The sign-in page

Mount `<SignIn>` from `@mortar-kit/admin` at `/admin/sign-in`. Give it `auth.methods` (from a server function) and the page's search parameters, which carry its state: an email sent, an emailed link, an error. It's plain forms, so it works without JavaScript. Pass the signed-in user to `<Admin user={...}>` to show them at the foot of the rail, with Sign out.

## Google

Create an OAuth client of type "Web application" in the Google Cloud console and add `https://your-site/admin/auth/google/callback` as a redirect URI. `hostedDomain: 'example.com'` accepts only that Workspace domain. Only verified addresses get through.

## Email

One email carries a 6-digit code and a link, so it works both on the device that asked and on a phone. Codes and links work once, expire after 15 minutes and allow five tries. The page says "check your email" whether or not the address may sign in, and sends nothing to addresses `authorize` refuses.

Email goes through a `Mailer`. `resendMailer({ apiKey, from })` sends through Resend (`authFromEnv` uses it when `RESEND_API_KEY` is set). Any other provider is one `send` function:

```ts
const mailer: Mailer = {
  async send(message) {
    await postmark.sendEmail({ From: 'admin@example.com', To: message.to, Subject: message.subject, TextBody: message.text, HtmlBody: message.html });
  },
};
```

`consoleMailer()` prints emails to the server console instead, for development. Pending sign-ins (hashes only) go in any `ContentStore`, under the reserved collection `mortar-auth-tokens`. With the JSON-file store, keep them outside your content and git.

## Behind a proxy

Set `baseUrl: 'https://example.com'` when the server sees a different origin than visitors do, so redirect URIs, email links and the same-origin check use the public one.

## Your own method

A method is `{ id, label, input, start, callback }`. `start` handles the sign-in form's post (redirect to a provider, send something). `callback` handles the return and resolves to `{ identity }`; `createAuth` does the rest (asks `authorize`, sets the cookie). Its context signs state (`ctx.sealer`), builds URLs and fails back to the sign-in page.
